description:
Change `draft-product`, capability `product-repository` (docs/changes/draft-product/capabilities/product-repository.md), requirements `draft` and `manifest`.

Build the library that creates a draft under a products home and reads a product folder's identity:
- the products home from `MADARCH_HOME`, then a given folder, else `~/madarch/products`;
- the folder `idea-YYYY-MM-DD` from the local date, `-2`, `-3` … when that day's names are taken;
- `git init` and one commit holding `AGENTS.md` (the constitution: what the repository is, what each folder is for, that documents are Markdown in git), `CLAUDE.md` holding `@AGENTS.md`, `workspace.yaml` (`schemaVersion`, `id` from `crypto.randomUUID()`, `name`), `.gitignore` ignoring `repos/`, and `docs/`, `model/`, `skills/`, `prototypes/` each kept by a `.gitkeep`; `repos/` exists and is untracked;
- reading a product folder back: its id, name and schema version; a folder with no manifest, a manifest with no `id`, and a schema version madarch does not know are each refused naming the file and the field.

Owned scope: `src/product/` (new) and the test files for it. Error text, log lines and file/line reporting follow the project's conventions; every error names the full path and the line where the reader knows it. No test writes into a real home: point the home at a scratch folder.

## Done when
Tests through the library, in a scratch home, show: a created draft is a git repository with exactly one commit holding the skeleton above and an untracked `repos/`; a second draft that day is `-2` with another id; the manifest reads back the id, the name and the schema version; a manifest without `id` and one with an unknown schema version are each refused naming the file and the field; no file is written outside the products home. `bun run check` and the new tests pass.

Change `draft-product`, capability `product-repository`, requirement `draft`.

The command itself, on top of rtr.1.1's library:
- `scripts/madarch.ts` as the entry, reached as `madarch` after `bun link` in a checkout and as `bun scripts/madarch.ts ARGS` without it; `package.json` gains the `bin` field;
- `madarch new [--home FOLDER] [--port N] [--no-open]`, `madarch --help` and `madarch --version`-style usage lines as the project's other commands have them;
- exit codes: 0 the draft was created; 2 an argument or the products home cannot be used; 1 git refused to commit; each naming what is wrong;
- printed lines: the product's folder and its id, on standard output, one fact per line.

This task creates the draft only. Starting a server or opening a browser is stage 2's task (rtr.2.3); `new` must not start anything yet, and `--port`/`--no-open` may be accepted and unused, or left out until then — say which in the report.

Owned scope: `scripts/madarch.ts`, `package.json`, `src/product/cli.ts` (new) and the test file for the command. Tests run the command as a child process with `MADARCH_HOME` at a scratch folder, the way `test/server-serve.test.ts` runs a command.

## Done when
`bun scripts/madarch.ts new` with `MADARCH_HOME` at a scratch folder creates the draft and prints the folder and the id; `--home` moves it; a missing option value, an unknown option and a home that cannot be used each exit 2 naming what is wrong; `bun run check` and the new tests pass.

Change `draft-product`, capability `product-wiki`, requirements `pages`, `live` and `app`.

Madarch's own thin app, in `wiki/app/` beside the Starlight template, with its own `package.json`, lockfile, `tsconfig.json`, `vite.config.ts`, `index.html` and build:
- React built by Vite to static files; the app reads the wiki's API and holds no document bytes itself;
- a home page naming the product and listing its pages, saying so plainly when it has none yet;
- a page at `/p/` plus its path within the product, showing its Markdown as formatted text (`react-markdown`, which keeps remark inside it and leaves raw HTML out, so no injection step is needed);
- a link to another page of the wiki opens that page; a link the wiki does not hold is shown as its text;
- the pages are asked for again every second, so a change on disk shows within two seconds without a reload and without restarting the server.

The app's own scripts: `build`, `check` (its own `tsc --noEmit`), and `test` (bun's runner over the app's test files, with happy-dom for a DOM). `wiki/app/dist` is ignored by git. The app is built by its own package so madarch's dependencies and lockfile stay untouched; the coordinator records the app's checks in CI and in the agent doc (rtr.2.4).

Owned scope: `wiki/app/`, the app's test files inside it, and the root `.gitignore` entry for its `dist/`.

## Done when
`bun install --frozen-lockfile && bun run check && bun test && bun run build` inside `wiki/app` passes in a clean checkout, and the app's tests show: the home page lists the fixture's pages (and says there are none when there are none), a page renders its Markdown as formatted text, a link to a page opens that page while a link the wiki does not hold is shown as text, and the open page is refetched when the revision changes.

Change `draft-product`, capability `product-wiki`, requirements `serve`, `app` and `result`.

The command that puts the wiki in the reader's hands, on top of rtr.2.1 and rtr.2.2:
- `madarch new` creates the draft (rtr.1.2) and then serves its wiki in the same command; `madarch serve [--product FOLDER]` serves an existing product, defaulting to the current folder, which is where the agent runs;
- the app's built files are built once on first use from `wiki/app` when they are absent, saying so before the build starts, and never rebuilt while they are there; a build that fails stops the command with exit 2 and the builder's output;
- the address is printed, then opened through the machine's opener: `MADARCH_BROWSER`, then `BROWSER`, then `xdg-open` on Linux and `open` on macOS; an opener that fails is a warning with the address, never the command's failure. The opener is injected in tests, so no test launches a browser;
- exit codes: 2 an argument, a product folder, a manifest, an address or an app build that cannot be used, each naming what is wrong; 1 a step that failed; the command serves until it is stopped.

Owned scope: `src/product-wiki/serve.ts` (new), `src/product-wiki/app.ts` (new), `scripts/madarch.ts`, and the test file for the command. Tests run the command with `MADARCH_HOME` and the app's folder at scratch paths, with an injected opener and a fake builder; one test serves a real fixture product on port 0 and reads its home page.

## Done when
Tests show: `madarch new` in a scratch home creates the draft, serves its home page at the printed address and calls the opener with that address; `madarch serve` in a product's root serves the same wiki; a missing product folder and an address already taken each exit 2 naming what is wrong; the app is built once across two runs; a failing builder exits 2 with what the builder said; the product's files and git status are unchanged afterwards. `bun run check` and the whole suite pass.

Change `draft-product`; the protocol's **What describes the present is kept true**.

The documents that describe madarch as it now is, and the CI that runs its checks:
- `AGENTS.md`: the `madarch` command and how it is reached (`bun link`, `bun scripts/madarch.ts`), the products home and `MADARCH_HOME`, and that the wiki app's checks live in `wiki/app` (`bun install --frozen-lockfile && bun run check && bun test && bun run build`);
- `docs/glossary.md`: the entries for **Product repository**, **Draft product**, **Products home**, **Manifest**, **Wiki app** and **Page**, and its **Wiki** entry, which now names both the served app and the static export;
- `docs/system/architecture.md`: the served wiki among the parts (the product repository and its pages, the app, the server's product mode), and how a product's document reaches a reader;
- `.github/workflows/ci.yml`: the app's own install, check, tests and build as a step, so a broken app fails CI;
- `.shady2k/integration.md`: its "Full stage checks" line and its "Checks and execution" section name the app's checks. Edit it only where this change made it false.

Every claim written is checked against the code as it stands after rtr.2.3; nothing is described that is not there, and no path or command that no longer exists stays. Keep each entry short: the glossary is one line per term.

Owned scope: `AGENTS.md`, `docs/glossary.md`, `docs/system/architecture.md`, `.github/workflows/ci.yml`, `.shady2k/integration.md`.

## Done when
`node .shady2k/checks/check-present.mjs --config .shady2k/config.json --base main` reports no new error; every command and path the edited documents name exists in the checkout; the glossary's new terms are the names the code and the change record use; the app's step in CI runs its install, check, tests and build.

A push from a linked worktree (git worktree add, as every feature run uses) sets core.bare = true in the shared .git/config, so the main checkout stops being a work tree ("fatal: this operation must be run in a work tree"). Seen 2026-09-29 after pushing feature/agent-model from /home/dev/repos/madarch-agent-model.

Cause, reproduced on a scratch repository: in a linked worktree git exports GIT_DIR to its hooks; .githooks/pre-push runs .shady2k/push.mjs, which starts check-present.mjs with that environment, and check-present.mjs runs `git init -q <tmp>` to test ignore rules. With GIT_DIR set, git init reinitialises the shared repository as bare instead of creating the temporary one. The copied check is the set's and stays verbatim; the fault is reported upstream separately if the owner agrees.

## Done when
- push.mjs starts every check with the GIT_* repository variables (GIT_DIR, GIT_WORK_TREE, GIT_INDEX_FILE, GIT_COMMON_DIR, GIT_PREFIX, GIT_OBJECT_DIRECTORY) removed;
- a test pushes from a linked worktree through the real hook and the shared config keeps core.bare = false; it fails without the fix;
- the owner's main checkout has core.bare = false again (restored by hand 2026-09-29).

Change server-views, requirement views/one-view (depth 1 part). A new way in beside the view set, for the server to call; the view set's pages and workspace stay byte-identical.

## Done when
- the scenarios depth-one-equals-page and one-view-likec4 pass as tests;
- for the reference system, the depth-1 Mermaid answer of the landscape and of every element with children equals its committed page in examples/reference-system/views/mermaid without its Up and Open lines, and the LikeC4 answer of each passes likec4 validate with exactly one view;
- an element that does not exist, or a depth below 1, is an error naming it, and normally the view is returned;
- the view of an element without children shows it with its neighbours;
- bun run views reproduces the committed views unchanged.

Change server-views, requirement views/one-view (depth above 1). Each shown element with children down to the asked depth is a frame around its own children; relations collapse to the deepest shown elements; table and labels by the same rules.

## Done when
- the scenario depth-two-frames passes as a test (flowchart parses, checkout-cart and checkout-ui framed inside checkout-web inside shop, payments outside shop);
- the LikeC4 answer of the same view at depth 2 passes likec4 validate and shows the modules;
- the reference system's landscape and ordering at depth 2 and 3 parse (Mermaid) and validate (LikeC4);
- depth 1 answers are unchanged (the first task's tests still pass).

Change server-views, capability server: requirements store, sources, survives-restart, and the server's start and logs. bun scripts/serve.ts --data <folder> [--host 127.0.0.1] [--port 4180]; one history file per source in the data folder; POST /models {source, commit, committedAt, model}; GET /sources; JSON refusals {error:{message, field?, accepted?}} with 400/404/409; one log line per request.

## Done when
- tests through a real server on a free port: repeat-send answers already stored and changes nothing; invalid-model is refused naming the element's path and kind; the same commit with another model or time is refused with 409 naming the commit; separate-graphs stores two sources each declaring core; list returns both in code point order with commit, commit time and stored time; restart on the same data folder lists the same sources;
- serve.ts without --data exits 2 naming it, and normally starts listening on 127.0.0.1;
- a request logs one line with method, path, status, source and milliseconds; a refusal adds its message.
- the server's graph is a named set of sources and its query engine is built from the assertions of every source the graph lists (one source per graph in this milestone, named after it): a test builds a graph from two sources' histories whose ids do not clash and gets the union's view, and normally a one-source graph answers that source alone;
- docs/system/architecture.md is written with the server's parts and the path to a product of several repositories (change server-views, Design and decisions).

Change server-views, capability server: requirements view and explains. POST /views {source, element?, depth?, format}, answered through stage 2's one-view rendering from the source's own query engine (built on first use, updated on every store), at now and the first state.

## Done when
- tests through a real server: domain-as-mermaid on the reference system (ordering, depth 1) returns text/markdown equal to the one-view answer; the LikeC4 answer passes likec4 validate; unknown-source answers 404 naming the source, listing the held sources and the send command with its arguments; unknown-element answers 404 naming element and source; a bad depth or format answers 400 naming the field and what is accepted; and normally each valid request answers 200;
- the same request twice, and after a restart, gives byte-identical text;
- a view of the reference system answers in under one second once its engine is built.

Change server-views, requirement model-authoring/send. The skill's last step, after a clean check: with a server address in the request or MADARCH_SERVER, run the send command and report the stored source and commit or the refusal; without one, say how to send later.

## Done when
- skills/write-intended-model/SKILL.md holds the step with the exact command and both branches (server named, none named);
- the skill's folder still needs nothing outside itself, madarch's checkout and the repository;
- run once by an agent with the skill on the reference system against a running server, its report names the source and commit the server stored.

Change server-views, check server-views. A scripted run (MADARCH_SERVER_E2E=1 or a script beside check-views) plus the GitHub render walk.

## Done when
- a server on a scratch data folder; the reference system and nocx's model (its local checkout at 3f0e46e) sent with the send command; for each, the landscape, one domain or service, and a core's modules requested as Mermaid and LikeC4;
- every Mermaid answer parses, and on GitHub's Mermaid renderer in a 1150 px column its labels are at least 12 px with no overlap (numbers recorded per answer); every LikeC4 answer passes likec4 validate;
- resending each commit answers already stored and all answers are byte-identical; a never-sent source is explained with the send command;
- the numbers are recorded on this task.

The plugin's setup version (0.37.0) is newer than the installed checks (0.33.0).

## Owner decisions (2026-09-29)
- Profile accepted: a pre-push hook checks every commit a push introduces for its task link; the present-documents check runs on push over AGENTS.md, the glossary and the current capability specs. Older drift it reports is filed as one debt item.
- Jev: consent given, route OpenRouter; the key's place is this machine's (~/.config/shady2k-skills/jev.json), never the config.
- Everything else retained (block strength, tdd, worker sessions, push landing, personal scope).

## Done when
- the four 0.37.0 checks are installed verbatim under .shady2k/checks with their format readers;
- the config carries presentDocuments and jev; the integration names the new entry points;
- .githooks/pre-push enumerates what each pushed ref introduces, passes a ref that introduces nothing, refuses an unlinked commit, a missing ref list and an uncomputable range, and runs the present-documents check against the push base;
- every setup proof passes (selftests, tracker counts, real entry points on a scratch remote, Jev status and mask, current gate) and the installation lands on main.
